item.description:
Score 3 | Comments 0 (<a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=31641062">thread link</a>) | @neosapien
<br/>
June 6, 2022 | https://ctrl-z.xyz/2022/06/dumb-coding/ | <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/*/https://ctrl-z.xyz/2022/06/dumb-coding/">archive.org</a>
<br/><!-- hnss:readable-content --><hr/><div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>Recently, I watched a talk from <a href="https://twitter.com/KevlinHenney">Kevlin Henney</a>,
about <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FyCYva9DhsI">clean code</a> and a lot of it resonated with me.</p>
<p>In his talk, he gives some pretty outrageous examples of <em>bad code</em>, and today, we are going to
analyze a specific snippet, and how awful it truly is. First for some context,
many thousands of websites crashed because of a dependency upon some obscure <code>npm</code> package.</p>
<p>This dependency wad on a simple <code>leftpad()</code> function, responsible for, as you can guess,
left-side padding. I won’t analyze the original, which was awful for other
reasons, but the replacement, that was supposed to be, “better.”</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>var</span> <span>cache</span> <span>=</span> [
  <span>''</span>,
  <span>' '</span>,
  <span>'  '</span>,
  <span>'   '</span>,
  <span>'    '</span>,
  <span>'     '</span>,
  <span>'      '</span>,
  <span>'       '</span>,
  <span>'        '</span>,
  <span>'         '</span>,
  <span>'          '</span>
];

<span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>str</span>, <span>len</span>, <span>ch</span>) {
  <span>// convert `str` to `string`
</span><span></span>  <span>str</span> <span>=</span> <span>str</span> <span>+</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// `len` is the `pad`'s length now
</span><span></span>  <span>len</span> <span>=</span> <span>len</span> <span>-</span> <span>str</span>.<span>length</span>;
  <span>// doesn't need to pad
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&lt;=</span> <span>0</span>) <span>return</span> <span>str</span>;
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>ch</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>ch</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>// convert `ch` to `string`
</span><span></span>  <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>ch</span> <span>+</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// cache common use cases
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>ch</span> <span>===</span> <span>' '</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>len</span> <span>&lt;</span> <span>10</span>) <span>return</span> <span>cache</span>[<span>len</span>] <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
  <span>// `pad` starts with an empty string
</span><span></span>  <span>var</span> <span>pad</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// loop
</span><span></span>  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `ch` to `pad` if `len` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>pad</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// divide `len` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>len</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `ch` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `len`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `ch` is "doubled", the `len` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// `len` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
    }
    <span>return</span> <span>pad</span> <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
}
</code></pre></div><h2 id="cache">Cache</h2>
<p>Wow, that’s hard to look at. Lets talk about the first thing that stands out, this <code>cache</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>var</span> <span>cache</span> <span>=</span> [
  <span>''</span>,
  <span>' '</span>,
  <span>'  '</span>,
  <span>'   '</span>,
  <span>'    '</span>,
  <span>'     '</span>,
  <span>'      '</span>,
  <span>'       '</span>,
  <span>'        '</span>,
  <span>'         '</span>,
  <span>'          '</span>
];
<span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>str</span>, <span>len</span>, <span>ch</span>) {
<span>// ...
</span><span></span>  <span>// cache common use cases
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>ch</span> <span>===</span> <span>' '</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>len</span> <span>&lt;</span> <span>10</span>) <span>return</span> <span>cache</span>[<span>len</span>] <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
<span>// ...
</span><span></span>}
</code></pre></div><p>The author of this code probably thought this would save computing time, making this more “efficient”.</p>
<p>This reasoning is bad for quite a few reasons:</p>
<ol>
<li>While usually we do often use a space character as the padding, sometimes we don’t and this unnecessary check, adds calculation
in all other cases</li>
<li>Why arbitrarily stop at 10 characters, first of all we allocate for all cases from 0 to 10 regardless of the size, effectively, this means you are
losing performance on any length less than 10.</li>
<li>Even without the two previous problems, it needlessly complicates the code, and as we shall see, the fact that later on the <code>while</code> loop attempts
to “cleverly” (not) lower the complexity to <code>O(log(n))</code>, this comes back to complexity in O(10) for any <code>n</code> lower than or equal to 10, thus his “optimisation”,
not only isn’t used in most cases, but is actually replaced with something with a complexity bigger than <code>O(n)</code></li>
</ol>
<p>Lets therefore begin by getting rid of all of that immediately.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>str</span>, <span>len</span>, <span>ch</span>) {
  <span>// convert `str` to `string`
</span><span></span>  <span>str</span> <span>=</span> <span>str</span> <span>+</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// `len` is the `pad`'s length now
</span><span></span>  <span>len</span> <span>=</span> <span>len</span> <span>-</span> <span>str</span>.<span>length</span>;
  <span>// doesn't need to pad
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&lt;=</span> <span>0</span>) <span>return</span> <span>str</span>;
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>ch</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>ch</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>// convert `ch` to `string`
</span><span></span>  <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>ch</span> <span>+</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// `pad` starts with an empty string
</span><span></span>  <span>var</span> <span>pad</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// loop
</span><span></span>  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `ch` to `pad` if `len` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>pad</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// divide `len` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>len</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `ch` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `len`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `ch` is "doubled", the `len` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// `len` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
    }
    <span>return</span> <span>pad</span> <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
}
</code></pre></div><h2 id="string-conversion">String Conversion</h2>
<p>Alright, so that’s done, but there’s still a lot of work to do, like the fact there are more comments than lines of code. Notice that for
a few lines, the comment is needed to explain what is happening, because unless you <strong>know</strong> about javascript’s type cohersion, you have no idea
why these lines are there or what they do, I’m of course talking about these:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>// convert `str` to `string`
</span><span></span><span>str</span> <span>=</span> <span>str</span> <span>+</span> <span>''</span>;
</code></pre></div><p>Let’s make the conversion explicit and thus also have errors reported if something shouldn’t work instead of simply transforming objects
into strings through cohersion. Also, since the conversion is explicit, no need for comments to explain it.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>str</span>, <span>len</span>, <span>ch</span>) {
  <span>str</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>str</span>)
  <span>// `len` is the `pad`'s length now
</span><span></span>  <span>len</span> <span>=</span> <span>len</span> <span>-</span> <span>str</span>.<span>length</span>;
  <span>// doesn't need to pad
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&lt;=</span> <span>0</span>) <span>return</span> <span>str</span>;
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>ch</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>ch</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>ch</span>)
  <span>// `pad` starts with an empty string
</span><span></span>  <span>var</span> <span>pad</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>// loop
</span><span></span>  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `ch` to `pad` if `len` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>pad</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// divide `len` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>len</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `ch` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `len`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `ch` is "doubled", the `len` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// `len` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
  }
  <span>return</span> <span>pad</span> <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
}
</code></pre></div>
<p>Alright, now let’s get rid of useless comments, my favourite is <code>// loop</code> before the <code>while</code>,
gee, thanks for letting us know!</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>str</span>, <span>len</span>, <span>ch</span>) {
  <span>str</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>str</span>)
  <span>// `len` is the `pad`'s length now
</span><span></span>  <span>len</span> <span>=</span> <span>len</span> <span>-</span> <span>str</span>.<span>length</span>;
  <span>// doesn't need to pad
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&lt;=</span> <span>0</span>) <span>return</span> <span>str</span>;
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>ch</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>ch</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>ch</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>ch</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>pad</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `ch` to `pad` if `len` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>pad</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// divide `len` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>len</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `ch` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `len`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `ch` is "doubled", the `len` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>len</span>) <span>ch</span> <span>+=</span> <span>ch</span>;
    <span>// `len` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
  }
  <span>return</span> <span>pad</span> <span>+</span> <span>str</span>;
}
</code></pre></div><h2 id="legibility">Legibility</h2>
<p>Let’s rename these variables, they are atrocious.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>content</span>, <span>length</span>, <span>padChar</span>) {
  <span>content</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>content</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>padLength</span> <span>=</span> <span>length</span> <span>-</span> <span>content</span>.<span>length</span>;
  <span>// doesn't need to pad
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span> <span>&lt;=</span> <span>0</span>) <span>return</span> <span>content</span>;
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>padChar</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>padChar</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>padChar</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>padding</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `padChar` to `padding` if `padLength` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>padding</span> <span>+=</span> <span>padChar</span>;
    <span>// divide `padLength` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>padLength</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `padChar` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `padLength`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `padChar` is "doubled", the `padLength` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span>) <span>padChar</span> <span>+=</span> <span>padChar</span>;
    <span>// `padLength` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
  }
  <span>return</span> <span>padding</span> <span>+</span> <span>content</span>;
}
</code></pre></div><p>Now making the names a little longer wasn’t stricly necessary, like <code>length</code> instead of <code>len</code>. However <code>ch</code> and <code>str</code> are <code>type</code> names, not
parameter names. They should describe the <em>purpose</em> of the content, not what it is.</p>
<p>I also made a new variable <code>padLength</code> because this:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js">  <span>// `len` is the `pad`'s length now
</span></code></pre></div><p>Is horrendous, it is a very bad idea to reuse a variable name, and give it a new purpose, or meaning, originally, <code>len</code> meant the length
of the <em>entire</em> string, now it means only the length of the <em>padding</em>. If there is a new purpose, make a new variable, it’s not like it’s reallocating
all your ram, this is all on the stack.</p>
<h2 id="fixing-the-handling-of-length">Fixing the handling of <code>length</code></h2>
<p>Speaking of this new declaration, there is no reason it should ever be negative, it only adds confusion when the next
<code>if</code> statement checks for that.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>content</span>, <span>length</span>, <span>padChar</span>) {
  <span>content</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>content</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>padLength</span> <span>=</span> Math.<span>max</span>(<span>length</span> <span>-</span> <span>content</span>.<span>length</span>, <span>0</span>)
  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>padChar</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>padChar</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>padChar</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>padding</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `padChar` to `padding` if `padLength` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>padding</span> <span>+=</span> <span>padChar</span>;
    <span>// divide `padLength` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>padLength</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" the `padChar` so this operation count grows logarithmically on `padLength`
</span><span></span>    <span>// each time `padChar` is "doubled", the `padLength` would need to be "doubled" too
</span><span></span>    <span>// similar to finding a value in binary search tree, hence O(log(n))
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span>) <span>padChar</span> <span>+=</span> <span>padChar</span>;
    <span>// `padLength` is 0, exit the loop
</span><span></span>    <span>else</span> <span>break</span>;
  }
  <span>return</span> <span>padding</span> <span>+</span> <span>content</span>;
}
</code></pre></div><h2 id="fixing-the-default-padchar">Fixing the “default” padChar</h2>
<p>There is another section of this code which particularly irks me:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js">  <span>// ch defaults to ' '
</span><span></span>  <span>if</span> (<span>!</span><span>padChar</span> <span>&amp;&amp;</span> <span>padChar</span> <span>!==</span> <span>0</span>) <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> <span>' '</span>;
  <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>padChar</span>)
</code></pre></div><p>For those who don’t understand this, it’s normal, you probably wonder why we are negating <code>padChar</code> with <code>!</code>, then
checking it is not equal to an integer <code>0</code>, before assigning it it’s default value. This is largely a byproduct of javascript’s
horrible type system, and it’s choice of dynamic typing, but let’s make it clearer:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>padChar</span> <span>||</span> <span>pad</span> <span>===</span> <span>0</span> <span>?</span> <span>pad</span> <span>:</span> <span>' '</span>)[<span>0</span>]
</code></pre></div><p>This is a conditional assignment, for those who don’t know, the form:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>var</span> <span>x</span> <span>=</span> <span>cond</span> <span>?</span> <span>val</span> <span>:</span> <span>otherVal</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Is a shorthand for</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>var</span> <span>x</span>;

<span>if</span> <span>cond</span> {
  <span>x</span> <span>=</span> <span>val</span>
} <span>else</span> {
  <span>x</span> <span>=</span> <span>otherVal</span>
}
</code></pre></div><p>(Note: this was also part of Kevlin Henney’s refactor)</p>
<h2 id="the-loop">The Loop</h2>
<p>So we’ve fixed a lot of the checking, and parametrizing, let’s now look at the main execution loop:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="js"><span>function</span> <span>leftpad</span>(<span>content</span>, <span>length</span>, <span>padChar</span>) {
  <span>content</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>content</span>)
  <span>var</span> <span>padLength</span> <span>=</span> Math.<span>max</span>(<span>length</span> <span>-</span> <span>content</span>.<span>length</span>, <span>0</span>)
  <span>padChar</span> <span>=</span> String(<span>padChar</span> <span>||</span> <span>pad</span> <span>===</span> <span>0</span> <span>?</span> <span>pad</span> <span>:</span> <span>' '</span>)[<span>0</span>]
  <span>var</span> <span>padding</span> <span>=</span> <span>''</span>;
  <span>while</span> (<span>true</span>) {
    <span>// add `padChar` to `padding` if `padLength` is odd
</span><span></span>    <span>if</span> (<span>padLength</span> <span>&amp;</span> <span>1</span>) <span>padding</span> <span>+=</span> <span>padChar</span>;
    <span>// divide `padLength` by 2, ditch the remainder
</span><span></span>    <span>padLength</span> <span>&gt;&gt;=</span> <span>1</span>;
    <span>// "double" …</span></code></pre></div></div></div>
<br/><p><em>Article truncated for RSS feed. Read the full article at <a href="https://ctrl-z.xyz/2022/06/dumb-coding/">https://ctrl-z.xyz/2022/06/dumb-coding/</a></em></p>

